course: biology
lesson: 1
title: What a habitat is: home, food, shelter and fitting in
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A habitat is the place where a living thing lives and finds everything it needs: the right air or water, food, shelter and a safe place to raise its young.
explain: Lift a stone in the garden and the woodlice underneath scurry away at once: they are not shy, they are drying out, because their damp, dark home under the stone is what keeps them alive.
explain: Every living thing is fitted to its own habitat, so a cactus is built for dry sand and a water lily for a still pond, and swapping them would kill both.
explain: A habitat can be huge like a rainforest or tiny like one rotting log, wild like a wood or made by people like a fish tank, a barn or a supermarket car park.
explain: In Tom and Jerry, Jerry lives in a hole in the wall and dreams of cheese; real house mice do live in the gaps of our houses, but they would much rather eat seeds, grain and crumbs than cheese.
fact: A habitat gives a living thing the right water or air, food, shelter and a safe place to raise young; a pet's keeper must supply all of these in a tank or a cage.
fact: Woodlice live in dark, damp places because they are crustaceans, cousins of crabs, with 14 legs (insects such as beetles have 6) and gill-like breathing parts that must stay moist, so dry sunny air can kill them within hours.
fact: European robins once followed wild boar and deer that churned up the soil; now they follow gardeners for worms, and both males and females sing in winter to defend a feeding territory.
fact: A hermit crab protects its soft, curled body inside an empty sea snail shell and moves into a bigger shell as it grows; crabs sometimes line up by size and swap shells in a chain.
fact: The idea that goldfish remember things for only 3 seconds is a myth: experiments show they remember feeding times and tricks for months.
fact: Grey squirrels bury thousands of nuts in autumn to eat in winter, and forgotten acorns can grow into new oak trees, so squirrels help plant their own woods.
fact: A cactus has thick, waxy, spiny stems that store water for the desert, while a water lily has big flat leaves that float on a pond.
fact: Red foxes now live in many towns, where they find food, shelter and few predators (animals that hunt them), eating scraps, worms and rats, and in some British cities there are more foxes per square kilometre than in the countryside.
fact: A single fallen tree can be home to hundreds of species of fungi, mosses, beetles, woodlice and centipedes, so nature reserves often leave dead wood lying.
fact: Stick insects hide from birds by looking like twigs and even swaying like a twig in the breeze; one from China measured about 64 cm with its legs stretched out.
fact: Barn swallows build cup nests of mud and grass under roofs, and those that nest in Britain fly about 10,000 km to southern Africa for the winter, often returning to the same barn.
fact: A frog needs two habitats: its tadpoles live in water and breathe with gills, while adult frogs hunt insects on damp land, so losing ponds means losing frogs.
fact: Koalas eat almost only eucalyptus leaves, which are tough, low in energy and slightly poisonous and need special gut bacteria to digest, so they rest or sleep up to about 20 hours a day and depend completely on their forest.
fact: House mice live in and around buildings and mainly eat seeds, grain and crumbs; the cartoon idea that mice love cheese above everything is not true.
exercise: What is a habitat? | The place where a living thing finds water or air, food, shelter and a safe place for its young | Any place that is wild and far from people, such as a deep forest, a high mountain or an empty beach | Only the nest or hole an animal sleeps in | A place where many kinds of animals are kept for people to see
exercise: Why do woodlice run to hide when you lift their stone? | Their breathing parts must stay damp, and dry sunny air can kill them | They are hunting for food under the next stone and want to get there first | They are frightened of the noise you make | Bright light makes them blind for ever, so they rush back into the dark
exercise: What kind of animal is a woodlouse? | A crustacean, a cousin of crabs | An insect, a close cousin of beetles | A small spider with a few extra legs | A baby centipede that has not grown up yet
exercise: Why does a robin wait on the spade handle while someone digs? | Digging turns up worms and grubs for it to snatch | It wants to keep the gardener company, like a pet dog | It is resting after a long migration | It is guarding its nest, which is hidden inside the handle of the spade
exercise: What does a hermit crab use as its shelter? | An empty sea snail shell that it swaps as it grows | A deep hole that it digs for itself in the wet sand | A hard shell that grows on its own back, like a snail | A piece of seaweed that it wraps round itself like a blanket
exercise: What must a goldfish tank give the fish? | Clean water, oxygen, food and places to hide | Only food, because fish need nothing else to stay well | Only water, because fish find their own food | Sunlight and soil, like a garden full of plants
exercise: How long can a goldfish really remember things? | For months, such as feeding times and tricks | For only about 3 seconds, then it forgets everything | For about 3 minutes | They cannot remember anything at all
exercise: Why does a grey squirrel bury acorns in autumn? | To store food for the winter, when food is scarce | To grow its own oak trees on purpose, like a gardener | To hide them from other squirrels for fun | Because acorns taste better after being buried in the cold soil for a while
exercise: How does a cactus fit its desert habitat? | Its thick, waxy stems store water and its spines guard it | Its big flat leaves float on water | Its roots go straight down to an underground river many kilometres deep | It drinks rain through its spines
exercise: Why have red foxes moved into towns and cities? | Towns give them food, shelter and few large predators | Foxes are scared of the countryside and its loud farm machines | Towns are colder than woods | Farmers chase all foxes into towns with dogs
exercise: Why is a rotting log a good habitat for small creatures? | It is soft, damp and full of food, so it is a home and a larder | It is dry and hot, like a desert | It has no food in it, so nothing comes there to eat the small creatures | It is poisonous to every predator
exercise: How does a stick insect stay safe in its habitat? | It looks and sways like a twig, so birds miss it | It bites birds hard with its strong, poisonous jaws | It flies off faster than any bird can ever follow | It makes a loud buzz that scares birds away from the bush
exercise: Where do barn swallows from Britain spend the winter? | In southern Africa, 10,000 km away | Asleep in the mud at the bottom of ponds | In the same barn, hiding up in the roof | In the Arctic, where it is light all day
exercise: Why does a frog need two habitats? | Its tadpoles live in water and the adult frog hunts on damp land | It moves house every month to fool predators | It lives in water by day and in tall trees by night | It needs one place for summer and another for its long winter sleep
exercise: Why do koalas sleep up to about 20 hours a day? | Eucalyptus leaves give little energy and are hard to digest | Koalas hunt all night and are tired | They are too heavy to move much | The heat of the zoo and the long sunny days make them very lazy
exercise: What do house mice mainly eat? | Seeds, grain and crumbs | Mostly cheese and some milk | Mostly insects and worms | Mostly leaves and green grass
exercise: How many legs does a woodlouse have? | 14 | 6 | 8 | 100
test: «lol tom and jerry, mice live for cheese right» What is right? | No, house mice mainly eat seeds, grain and crumbs, though they will nibble cheese | Yes, cheese is the main food of every mouse | Yes, mice cannot live without cheese | No, mice only eat meat
test: Ellie swaps the plants in the garden centre: the cactus into the pond, the water lily into a pot of dry sand. What happens? | Both die: the cactus rots and the water lily shrivels | Both grow better, because they get a change of home | Only the water lily dies | Both slowly change into the other kind of plant over a few weeks
test: «found a woodlouse, gonna keep it in a jar on the windowsill in the sun» What is the best advice? | Give it damp soil, leaves and shade, or it will dry out and die | Sun is fine, it is an insect and likes heat | Give it a dish of cheese and nothing else | Put it in a dry jar so it does not drown in the damp
test: «my nan says goldfish forget everything in 3 seconds so they never get bored» What is right? | Wrong: goldfish remember feeding times and tricks for months | Right: three seconds is all they remember | Right, and that is why they do not need a filter or anything to do | Wrong: they remember for exactly three minutes
test: What would happen to hermit crabs if people took every empty shell home from the beach? | Crabs would have nowhere safe to grow into, and many would be eaten | Hermit crabs would grow their own shells | Nothing, they live happily without shells | They would move into sand holes instead with no trouble at all, and be just as safe
test: Dad wants to tidy all the dead wood out of the nature reserve. What is the best answer? | It is a home for hundreds of species, so it is better left lying | Good idea, dead wood has nothing living in it | Good idea, it makes the forest grow faster and keeps the trees healthy | It does not matter, nothing uses dead logs
test: «sod it, mate says a city's not a real habitat, only wild places count» What is right? | A city is a habitat too: foxes, pigeons and mice find food and shelter there | He's right, animals only live in wild places | He's right, city animals are all pets that escaped from their homes long ago | A city counts only for insects
test: «we forgot loads of acorns lol, squirrels must be well thick» What is the clever answer? | Forgotten acorns grow into new oaks, so squirrels help plant their own woods | Squirrels never forget a single nut | Forgotten acorns are always eaten by worms | Squirrels bury acorns only to grow trees on purpose, like careful gardeners do
test: «the school pond's getting filled in for car parking, who cares» What is the best answer? | The frogs lose the water they need for eggs and tadpoles | Nothing changes, frogs can lay eggs on grass | Tadpoles can live in puddles on the car park until they grow up | Frogs only need ponds when they are old
test: «why'd the zoo need to grow eucalyptus just for the koalas, give 'em lettuce» What is right? | Koalas eat almost only eucalyptus and need special gut bacteria for it | Koalas would happily eat lettuce instead | Koalas eat meat, and the eucalyptus leaves are only there for bedding | Any tree leaves will do for a koala
test: Count them up: a woodlouse and a beetle sit on the same log. How many legs together? | 20 | 12 | 14 | 22
test: «that swallow's been in our barn every summer, where's it go in winter, the attic?» What is right? | It flies about 10,000 km to southern Africa and often comes back | It sleeps all winter in the barn roof | It hides in the mud of the pond | It dies in the autumn, and a brand new swallow comes to the barn each year
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a habitat? | The place in which an organism obtains the water or air, food, shelter and conditions for rearing young that it requires | Any region remote from human settlement, irrespective of whether an organism may obtain its requirements there | Exclusively the nest or burrow in which an animal sleeps | An enclosure in which animals are exhibited to the public
exam: Identify the principal reason for which woodlice are confined to damp and shaded situations. | Their respiratory structures must remain moist, and exposure to dry air may prove fatal within hours | They are unable to perceive light of any description and therefore remain wherever they first find themselves | They subsist exclusively upon stones | They are incapable of locomotion in daylight
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the memory of the goldfish. | The goldfish is capable of retaining feeding times and learned tasks for a period of months | The memory of the goldfish is limited to approximately three seconds, after which all is forgotten | The goldfish possesses no capacity for memory whatsoever | The memory of the goldfish is limited to approximately three minutes
exam: Ascertain the consequence which would ensue were a cactus and a water lily to exchange their respective habitats. | Both plants would perish, the former through rotting and the latter through desiccation | Both plants would flourish in consequence of the change and the greater variety of conditions | The cactus alone would perish | Each plant would in time be transformed into the other
